intro.bodyMarkdown:
When there are more than two cases, chain them with `else if`. JavaScript tests each condition in order and runs the **first** block whose condition is true; the rest are skipped. A final `else` catches everything else.

```
const score = 74
if (score >= 90) {
  console.log("A")
} else if (score >= 70) {
  console.log("B")
} else if (score >= 50) {
  console.log("C")
} else {
  console.log("Try again")
}
```

Because only the first match runs, the order matters. With `score >= 50` first, a score of 95 would get a C. Put the most specific (or highest) case first.

Each `else if` is only reached when the conditions before it were false, so you rarely need to repeat them: after `score >= 90` fails, the next test can just be `score >= 70`, without `&& score < 90`.

Several separate `if`s are different from a chain: each one is checked, so more than one can run.

```
const n = 12
if (n % 2 === 0) {
  console.log("even")
}
if (n % 3 === 0) {
  console.log("divides by 3")
}
```

