intro.bodyMarkdown:
Two keywords change how a loop runs. `break` stops the loop straight away, and the program carries on after it. It is handy for searching: once you have found what you need, there is no point looking further.

```
for (let i = 1; i <= 100; i++) {
  if (i * i > 50) {
    console.log("First square over 50:", i * i)
    break
  }
}
```

`continue` skips the rest of this run and goes on to the next one. It keeps the main code un-indented when some cases should simply be left out.

```
for (let i = 1; i <= 6; i++) {
  if (i % 3 === 0) {
    continue
  }
  console.log(i)
}
```

With `break`, `while (true)` becomes useful: a loop that runs until something inside it decides to stop. Make sure some path always reaches the `break`.

```
let n = 1
while (true) {
  n *= 3
  if (n > 100) break
}
console.log(n)
```

Both only affect the loop they are in. (`break` also ends a `switch`, as you have seen.)

