intro.bodyMarkdown:
A loop can go inside another loop. For each run of the outer loop, the inner loop runs all the way through. That gives every combination: rows and columns, pairs of numbers, a grid.

```
for (let row = 1; row <= 3; row++) {
  for (let col = 1; col <= 2; col++) {
    console.log(`row ${row}, col ${col}`)
  }
}
```

That shows 6 lines: 3 rows, each with 2 columns. The inner loop needs its own counter name (`col`, or `j`), separate from the outer one.

To draw a grid, build each row as text in the inner loop and show it once the row is done:

```
for (let r = 1; r <= 3; r++) {
  let line = ""
  for (let c = 1; c <= 4; c++) {
    line += "#"
  }
  console.log(line)
}
```

The inner loop can depend on the outer counter. With `c <= r`, row 1 has one `#`, row 2 has two and so on: a triangle.

The work multiplies: 100 outer runs with 100 inner runs each is 10,000 runs. That is fine here, but it is worth knowing.

